sections.body:
A class can **inherit** everything from another class and add new things on top:

```python
class Hero:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

class Wizard(Hero):
    def cast(self):
        return f"{self.name} cast a spell!"

w = Wizard("Merlin")
print(w.cast())
```

`Wizard` uses `Hero`'s `__init__` method automatically. If you write its own `__init__`, you can call the parent's with `super().__init__(name)`.

examples.code:
class Ship:
    def __init__(self, name, cargo):
        self.name = name
        self.cargo = cargo

    def __str__(self):
        return f"The ship {self.name}, cargo: {self.cargo}"

print(Ship("Seagull", 12))

class Hero:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name
        self.hp = 100

class Wizard(Hero):
    def __init__(self, name):
        super().__init__(name)
        self.mana = 30

    def cast(self):
        self.mana -= 10
        return f"{self.name} cast a spell, mana {self.mana}"

w = Wizard("Merlin")
print(w.hp)
print(w.cast())

